WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.360
A cell checks new proteins before sending them on.

2
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.300
In the endoplasmic reticulum, or ER, secreted and membrane proteins get help

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.700
folding. A protein that keeps failing is held back, then sent to the cytosolic

4
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.740
proteasome through ER-associated degradation.

5
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:22.940
Why does this matter?

6
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:29.020
Proteins misfold. The cell needs to destroy the failures instead of sending them on

7
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.960
to the Golgi. Folding help and disposal work together to keep that protein

8
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:34.960
checkpoint running.

9
00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:37.633
BiP helps a new chain fold.

10
00:00:38.673 --> 00:00:42.293
Calnexin and calreticulin hold proteins with a glucose tag.

11
00:00:43.173 --> 00:00:48.633
If a released protein is still not folded, UGGT adds glucose back for another round.

12
00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:54.793
Persistent failures leave by the degradation route: ubiquitin tags the protein, an

13
00:00:54.793 --> 00:00:57.793
ATPase pulls it out, and the proteasome destroys it.

14
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:01.117
The proteasome destroys single proteins.

15
00:01:02.037 --> 00:01:04.837
For whole organelles, the cell uses the lysosome.

16
00:01:05.637 --> 00:01:10.677
In macroautophagy, a membrane cup grows around cytoplasm and closes into an

17
00:01:10.677 --> 00:01:12.537
autophagosome with two membranes.

18
00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:15.037
It fuses with a lysosome.

19
00:01:15.777 --> 00:01:18.337
The inner membrane and its contents are digested.

20
00:01:19.157 --> 00:01:21.697
The building blocks return to the cytosol for reuse.

21
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.490
What if unfolded proteins pile up in the ER?

22
00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:31.210
The unfolded protein response slows protein synthesis and makes more folding

23
00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.990
machinery. Mammals have three branches.

24
00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.330
IRE1 splices XBP1 messenger RNA.

25
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:40.850
PERK slows most translation.

26
00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.270
ATF6 travels to the Golgi to be cut.

27
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.130
Unresolved stress can drive cell death.

28
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.220
Autophagy can also choose its cargo.

29
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.500
Receptors link particular cargo to the LC3 family.

30
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.280
Mitophagy removes mitochondria.

31
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.020
In a well-studied route in cultured cells, PINK1 builds up on depolarized

32
00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.100
mitochondria and recruits Parkin.

33
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.480
Parkin adds ubiquitin tags.

34
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.920
Receptors bind the tagged mitochondrion and recruit the machinery that engulfs it.

35
00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:15.870
Finally, how do you measure autophagy?

36
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.250
More autophagosomes could mean more are made, or fewer are destroyed.

37
00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.130
Compare LC3-II with and without bafilomycin.

38
00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:29.250
Assuming a complete lysosomal block, the difference reveals what would otherwise

39
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.250
have been destroyed.

40
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.330
Check cargo too. LC3 marks the carrier.

41
00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:39.690
Read it alongside cargo measurements and fluorescent reporters to judge turnover.
